  Dropout is a regularization technique for artificial neural networks.
  It reduces overfitting by randomly disabling a subset of neurons during training.
  Each neuron is kept with probability p = 1 − r, where r is the dropout rate (0 < r < 1).
  Dropped neurons have their output set to zero for that forward pass.
  To preserve the expected activation magnitude, the outputs of kept neurons are scaled by 1 / p (inverted dropout).
  The random disabling is performed independently for each neuron and each training iteration.
  Dropout forces the network to learn redundant, robust features that do not rely on any single neuron.
  It prevents complex co‑adaptations between neurons, encouraging more general representations.
  During inference, dropout is turned off and all neurons are used, typically with their weights unchanged.
  The scaling applied during training ensures that the expected sum of activations remains the same at test time.
  Typical dropout rates are 0.2–0.5 for input layers and up to 0.5 for hidden layers.
  Variants such as SpatialDropout drop entire feature maps in convolutional layers.
  DropConnect randomly drops individual connections instead of whole neurons.
  Dropout can be combined with other regularizers like weight decay for stronger effect.
  Modern architectures sometimes replace dropout with batch normalization, but dropout remains useful in many settings.
  The algorithm is simple to implement and adds little computational overhead.
  In practice, dropout improves generalization on tasks such as image classification and language modeling.
  The technique was introduced by Srivastava et al. in 2014.
  It is supported by major deep‑learning libraries (TensorFlow, PyTorch, Keras) as a built‑in layer.
  Overall, dropout provides a cheap, effective way to make neural networks less prone to overfitting.
